"""Supplementary narrative; numerical tables are generated from verified records."""
BLOCKS=[]
def S(identifier,kind,text='',**kwargs):BLOCKS.append(dict(id=identifier,kind=kind,en=text,**kwargs))

S('s1','h1','S1 Specimen records and evaluation populations')
S('s1a','p','The input version contains 18 workbooks and 1,672 physical specimen records. The time-domain, frequency-domain, absorption and refractive-index sheets are linked views, not replicate specimens. Labels distinguish Zaoren from Suanzaoren throughout the analysis. Frozen specimen roles total TRAIN999, CAL334 and PRED339. The five Suanzaoren source workbooks supply 300 TRAIN, 100 CAL and 100 PRED records. The paired optical modelling grid contains 612 coordinates within 0.1–1.5 THz. Source workbook identifiers, labels and original bytes are preserved.')
S('s1_table','table',key='workbooks')
S('s1b','p','The Zaoren workbooks contain 350,5,3,3 and 3 specimens. Consequently, its fixed-role source task has 70 of 74 PRED specimens in the majority source. Accuracy, macro-F1 and balanced accuracy answer different questions in that task. Each Suanzaoren source is represented by one workbook; holding out that workbook also removes its source class. Closed-set source generalization to new batches therefore requires independently acquired examples of every source class in training and evaluation. The present LOWO analyses concern material classes, each represented by several workbooks.')

S('s2','h1','S2 Optical definitions and exported numerical scales')
S('s2a','p','The analysis uses the existing α and n exports, with no independent reconstruction of their software extraction. For physical notation, a conventional homogeneous-slab approximation relates a corrected effective equivalent-air-reference transfer function T to refractive index n and absorption coefficient α as follows. With unwrapped reference-minus-sample phase Φ, the corresponding index and absorption definitions are given in Eqs. (S1)–(S3). The source reference is Duvillaret, Garet and Coutaz, cited in the main manuscript.')
S('s2eq1','equation',key='optical_transfer')
S('s2eq2','equation',key='optical_index')
S('s2eq3','equation',key='optical_absorption')
S('s2b','p','Here ω=2πf, d is thickness and c is the speed of light. These expressions assume an effective plane-parallel slab near normal incidence, with internal reflections neglected or handled separately. T in these definitions is not an uncorrected sample/polyethylene ratio. Consistent SI lengths would give α in m⁻¹, but this dimensional statement does not establish the numerical unit of the existing export. The manuscript therefore retains its original exported scale. Nominal header thicknesses are not substituted for measured values, and refractive-index values are not adjusted toward a presumed organic-material range. These equations define quantities; they do not certify an undocumented extraction implementation.')

S('s3','h1','S3 Estimator settings and a reusable selection interface')
S('s3a','p','The principal selection scheme uses per-spectrum SNV with sample standard deviation (ddof=1), followed by training-fitted StandardScaler. Each class-stratified draw samples 80% of the current fitting class without replacement, rounding upward. Predictor columns and one-versus-rest responses are standardized with population standard deviations for the two-component PLS1 fits. The MC20 score combines absolute mean coefficients divided by their sample standard deviations using an across-class Euclidean norm. The denominator floor follows the main-text definition. Neither the outer labels nor the full collection determine ranks or scaler statistics.')
S('s3b','p','Eligible primary K values are 31,62,123,184 and 245. RBF-SVM C values are 0.1,1,10 and 100, and kernel scale factors are 1,0.1 and 10 in that tie order. Scale uses the actual selected fitting matrix after any block weighting. Joint SNV transforms n and α separately before concatenation. The raw-n/SNV-α weighted variant selects the n-block multiplier from 1,0.5 and 0.25. Equal-block selection has separate rankings and therefore a distinct ranking cost. MC100 changes the number of coefficient draws; five-stream MC20 aggregates independently generated rankings. The unchanged original configuration tables retain all eligible alternatives.')
S('s3_interface','table',key='interface')
S('s3c','p','The reusable component is the training-only ranking and coordinate-projection interface. It accepts a labelled fitting matrix and returns scores and selected coordinates; the final decision rule is supplied separately. To use it with a different classifier, preprocessing, ranking, K and that classifier’s eligible settings must stay inside the appropriate validation loop. An SVM-selected K is not automatically optimal for LDA, an MLP or another estimator. This separation is an architectural capability of the workflow. The measured selection gain reported here uses SVM; the full-spectrum LDA and MLP rows do not constitute a cross-classifier test of selected features.')
S('s3_primary','table',key='all_primary')
S('s3d','p','Shrinkage LDA selects its eligible raw/SNV preprocessing and uses automatic shrinkage. The saved MLP reference selects raw/SNV preprocessing and 32/64 hidden units, with Adam, learning rate 0.001, L2 alpha 0.01, batch size 32, maximum 300 iterations, early stopping on 20% of the current fitting subset, patience 15 and random seed 42. Outer fitting therefore includes an early-stopping holdout of approximately 48 of 240 specimens; an inner 120-specimen fit includes approximately 24. Those holdouts do not receive gradient updates, although they remain inside the outer-training population.')
S('s3_nn','table',key='mlp_diagnostics')
S('s3e','p','The 15 saved MLP checkpoints reproduce their recorded outer predictions. They stop after 20–42 iterations, median 29, rather than at the maximum iteration count. Mean replay accuracy on the entire outer-fitting set is 93.06%, including the early-stopping holdout; mean outer-test accuracy is 73.22%. The replay is not a pure gradient-training score, and zero convergence warnings do not establish complete hyperparameter optimization. The selected-SVM comparison changes both features and estimator fitting; the same-budget full-spectrum SVM supplies a more informative reference for the observed gap. These checks do not prove that the implementation is bug-free or that a bug caused the lower score.')
S('s3_e1','table',key='e1_parameters')
S('s3f','p','The E1 full-spectrum bridge preserves all 15 original outer partitions and every original inner fitting and validation ID. All 360 inner fits reach the three distinct numerical gamma values, and the 15 outer refits complete the recorded 375 fits. Their parameters and pooled predictions are supplied in the data workbook. This bridge is a new execution on previously exposed data, not a new independent confirmation set. Feature-search and total execution costs are not equated with the bridge merely because classifier grids and specimen IDs match.')

S('s4','h1','S4 Feature budgets and selection repeatability')
S('s4a','p','Prescribed-budget analyses use the saved V4 candidate families and retain inner SVM-parameter selection. At K62, SNV absorption, joint SNV and raw absorption achieve 81.00%,87.11% and 88.78%. The complete budget table retains all stored families, not just the three curves in Fig. S1. The primary estimate instead uses K selected inside each outer-training subset. All 930 retained joint-SNV feature occurrences at K62 are absorption coordinates; these occurrences are selections, not 930 independent specimens or distinct frequencies.')
S('s4_figure','figure',key='S1')
S('s4_budgets','table',key='budget_core')
S('s4b','p','The mean cross-pipeline K62 Jaccard index, 0.0111, compares corresponding joint and absorption-only masks. Within-pipeline stability uses pairs of masks generated by the same method. Primary SNV absorption gives mean K220.6, Jaccard0.8110 and Φ0.8360 across 15 outer refits. Its fixed-K62 sensitivity gives Jaccard0.6952 and Φ0.7976. The candidate universe remains the full612 coordinates. A joint selector has1224 candidates; a conditional absorption-projection summary and a complete-joint-space Φ are different estimands and are not substituted for one another.')
S('s4_stability','table',key='stability')
S('s4c','p','The fixed-training experiment uses the first three complete saved contexts in its frozen order, fold0 of each; training IDs stay fixed within context. Thirty predetermined Monte Carlo seeds, 2026111600–2026111629, produce two rankings per task. The saved experiment contains90 paired tasks,180 rankings and18000 PLS fits, with no classifier training. Its plain K62 absorption Jaccard is0.7317–0.7663 and Φ0.8253–0.8504. The original source summary mislabels this variation as training-subset variation; the derived tables use the actual design, Monte Carlo-seed variation with training specimens fixed. The raw source bytes remain preserved.')
S('s4_mc','table',key='mc_stability')
S('s4d','p','Fixed-gap4 masks are less repeatable at exact coordinates: absorption Jaccard0.3413–0.3578 and Φ0.4236–0.4480 across the three training contexts. These masks arise from the same scores under a spacing constraint; the ranking experiment alone provides no accuracy result. Grouping4,8 or16 grid points is retained as a sensitivity description. Such widths are not established instrument resolution or chemical peak widths. Correlated neighboring coordinates permit substitution, so exact-coordinate agreement and group agreement answer different questions.')
S('s4e','p','In the separate crossed-mask assay, 15 fixed contexts reproduce all original diagonal predictions. Exchanging absorption masks changes mean accuracy by5.22 percentage points at either available parameter setting, with13 positive and2 negative contexts. C is100 for both arms in every context. Numerical gamma values are equal except for floating-point rounding, and exchanging these parameters changes no class prediction. The parameter factor is therefore not identifiable in those contexts. This is a conditional mask-substitution result at effectively common settings, not proof that hyperparameters are irrelevant, and its distinct split design is not combined numerically with the V4 K62 comparison.')
S('s4_cross','table',key='crossed')
S('s4_effect','table',key='paired_effects')

S('s5','h1','S5 Workbook-held-out material and fixed-role source evaluation')
S('s5a','p','LOWO uses all eligible specimens and preserves each complete workbook as a test group once. Fitting uses the remaining workbooks, SNV, a training-fitted scaler, all612 absorption coordinates and fixed balanced RBF-SVM parameters C10/gamma scale. It does not use an adaptive MC20 selector. Classifier parameters are not selected on the held-out workbook. All28 folds, including failed folds, appear below; pooled metrics count every held-out specimen once.')
S('s5_lowo','table',key='lowo_folds')
S('s5b','p','Binary LOWO gives854/864 correct,98.84% accuracy,98.63% balanced accuracy and macro-F10.9881. Four-material LOWO gives1097/1672 correct,65.61% accuracy,63.13% balanced accuracy and macro-F10.6143. Per-material recalls are Suanzaoren99.60%,Huangqi80.65%,Zaoren45.60% and Tianma26.67%. Equal-weight workbook mean accuracy is a different estimand and does not replace the pooled score. Fixed-role results use a different population and training composition, so their difference from LOWO is not interpreted as a paired causal effect or as proof of leakage.')
S('s5c','p','Source-task fixed PRED results are Suanzaoren85/100,Zaoren72/74,Huangqi69/82 and Tianma81/83. The Zaoren majority-source baseline is70/74, while its balanced accuracy is60.00% and macro-F10.5972. Different tasks share specimens and have different class structures. Their results are not independent confirmations or a ranking of task difficulty. Repeatedly developing methods on this collection also leaves historical model-selection exposure; neither repeated partitions nor nested hyperparameter selection retrospectively removes that history.')

S('s6','h1','S6 Support-label controls and neural transfer references')
S('s6a','p','The B1–B5 studies retain all corrected positive and negative results. Their105,345,375,375 and180 task bundles use overlapping data and partially shared endpoints; they are not five independently acquired cohorts. The five-source controls reported in the main text use the same300 Suanzaoren TRAIN specimens with190 Source,50 Support and60 Query specimens per context. K-shot1,3,5 and10 means5,15,25 and50 total labels. It must not be confused with a separate total-label acquisition budget.')
S('s6_support','table',key='support')
S('s6b','p','Complete refitting at1,3,5 and10 labels per class achieves80.00%,80.67%,82.11% and83.89%. At the largest budget,12 of15 contexts improve,2 tie and1 declines relative to the source model; at1 or3 labels per class,4 contexts decline. Source-scaler/numerical-gamma-frozen refitting gives83.78%, unlabelled-statistics updating80.11%, cyclically incorrect-label refitting73.33%, and correct-label refitting with normalized total loss mass83.78%. Increasing source-only loss mass remains80.00%. These observations support a valid-supervision effect within the stated full-spectrum task.')
S('s6c','p','B5 Source-internal strategy selection gives83.67%, compared with83.78% for direct frozen-transform refitting. Score motion does not establish useful adaptation: the weak-calibration endpoint changes all900 score vectors but only2 predicted classes. Label-acquisition variants retain their actual total budgets and all random controls in the source workbooks; their performance depends on the subsequent learner. These controls do not certify a combined selected-feature/adaptation pipeline that has not been evaluated as a whole.')
S('s6d','p','The fixed-PRED three-seed neural comparison gives81.00% target-only,80.33% additional-target-pretraining and79.00% auxiliary-material-pretraining mean accuracy. The network uses an interpolated128-point absorption representation and a residual architecture distinct from the nested full612-point MLP. Complete corrected1305 and V4 additional330 summaries remain in the data workbook, with original protocol and seed definitions. Continuations, backups, repeated predictions and fit counts are not added to the physical specimen count.')

S('s7','h1','S7 Evidence provenance and implementation scope')
S('s7a','p','The paper uses the explicitly allowlisted corrected-kind publication view and the immutable 16 November focused-followup snapshot 5594d9c3326fcd607ee72fb7fc93895047de8f8a. V4 and B1–B5 keep their individual immutable sources. The data workbook Source_Index and EVIDENCE_MANIFEST.json identify source commits, file hashes, eligible scope and derived-table hashes. The182 current-view files,18 original workbooks, E1 prediction identities, primary predictions and feature masks were checked. The original B1–B5 Excel data are preserved byte for byte. Additional interval and stability summaries use the saved paired predictions and coordinate masks.')
S('s7b','p','The later expanded-search worker is separate from the V4 and E1 implementations. Its transformation cache reused the first candidate’s numerical gamma, causing the declared three-factor search to act as a fixed-first-factor search. Source inspection and equal candidate-score vectors confirm the specific scope of this defect. Expanded-worker performance and cost claims are not used for the main algorithm comparison. Their historical outputs remain available with that qualification; a local patch or a dispatch-only regression check is not reported as a completed corrected scientific rerun. The crossed-mask assay is described at its executed, effectively common numerical settings.')
S('s7c','p','The paired primary intervals were recomputed consistently from saved same-ID predictions using10000 source-stratified physical-ID bootstrap draws, seed20261116. Each selected physical ID retains all3 repeated predictions; class counts remain fixed. Accordingly, these intervals differ in definition from older archived500-draw or5000-draw calculations and are not silently substituted into those protocols. They are conditional, retrospective and unadjusted. No macro-F1 interval, noninferiority margin, acquisition-time saving, independent-batch claim or unmeasured chemical assignment is inferred from them.')
S('s7d','p','The accompanying source workbooks contain the complete corrected alternatives, including low-performing references. Workbook splits and material labels remain separate from source-discrimination splits. Physical specimen IDs and the original TRAIN/CAL/PRED assignments remain available as provenance fields throughout the protocol-specific analyses.')

OPTICAL_EQUATIONS={
 'optical_transfer':r'T(\omega)=\frac{4n(\omega)}{[n(\omega)+1]^2}\exp\!\left[-\frac{\alpha(\omega)d}{2}\right]\exp\!\left[-i\frac{[n(\omega)-1]\omega d}{c}\right]',
 'optical_index':r'n(\omega)=1+\frac{c\Phi(\omega)}{\omega d}',
 'optical_absorption':r'\alpha(\omega)=-\frac{2}{d}\ln\!\left\{|T(\omega)|\frac{[n(\omega)+1]^2}{4n(\omega)}\right\}',
}
